#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4748324b-8edf-5fba-90e1-90683887d463" :question/math-academy-id "e-5436" :question/problem "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/343c9e46cf59cf4372a563c80dd9010bcece8c04f8babdb95d57c04cdf4a2503.png) \nWhat are the images of the standard basis vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot\\mathbf{i}$ and $T\\cdot\\mathbf{j}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "0db64243-ffa4-595b-8555-5ee369450557" :question/math-academy-id "e-5437" :question/problem "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/4d1b9e56c0c6214d949d12f45fe4aecea5516971203724ecf573a2b17c6e959a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/ccf1d5e4e5f040782f606fcccc9e183d92a640d4b5148602ce54b3e75ed116a5.png) and\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/7746aad7a72ef32c38d2851200542f13c7ca8c2c805580b2cee4f78bbd1d0bed.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/0dfa1b41bac9f0d8250950b3fc538799b1f94271793aef7c13f3f7ce6aaf53ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "ae7884f7-abb9-56ba-b685-f0eab27ff795" :question/math-academy-id "e-5438" :question/problem "Given that $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}, \\mathbf k\\}$ is the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the images of $\\mathbf{i},$ $\\mathbf{j},$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ under the action of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ are\n$\\langle 3, 5, -1 \\rangle,$ $\\langle 0, 4,3 \\rangle,$ and $\\langle -1, 5, -2\\rangle,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j},$ and $\\mathbf k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/7a94c68b63e914bc3bbc64fb22bfe85ad60746bd2f35cd1dce877f979067c39a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e17729e-6246-5492-abac-a85c68eeb205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232} {:answer/id #uuid "eecd1ca3-ce35-5367-b180-968888c0bb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345233} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf53d59-3e2c-5484-a11c-53f35c010dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345234} {:answer/id #uuid "92a89961-98ba-54ff-9d91-7f2c1df2d80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345235} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3c7a7a-c3db-584f-babd-e533be25293a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3c7a7a-c3db-584f-babd-e533be25293a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea33c882-d1e4-547a-8296-d98a1883b697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe5b7b5f-4b76-5de5-9aee-91aaaa5c11eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-139988" :question/problem "Given that ${i,j}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨-1,2⟩$ and $⟨3,-8⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ca763c-fad3-532e-8a94-34c596979eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345239} {:answer/id #uuid "27fdd6a0-0d99-56e6-aa30-bbba825a1b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345240} {:answer/id #uuid "4c86a5a8-6651-5704-8297-bbfdc1a79711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241} {:answer/id #uuid "15d0b5bd-50d2-5a2e-88ea-546b67137655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345242} {:answer/id #uuid "6866e88e-8177-587b-957e-19230d3bf12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c86a5a8-6651-5704-8297-bbfdc1a79711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33bbf2f-00fa-597e-b1b9-b0392614807b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79f4de40-5ae7-517c-ba73-7ba072ef9085" :question/math-academy-id "q-139990" :question/problem "Given that ${i,j,k}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨3,2,-1⟩,$ $⟨2,-3,1⟩,$ and $⟨1,-2,3⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee5d5f2-17c6-56ef-8906-b9a99e8c0b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345246} {:answer/id #uuid "e6dfda87-3d29-5703-9c7f-753637bd501d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247} {:answer/id #uuid "c12cd3f1-5c89-5ab9-a657-b9d9a56c0c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345248} {:answer/id #uuid "5af7fceb-8449-5d87-a57e-4ca096c2d24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345249} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bf5b58-49fb-5aa1-89a8-99d4a31615a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6bf5b58-49fb-5aa1-89a8-99d4a31615a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345250} :answer-field/id #uuid "69a306a4-178b-5710-b530-867ff40423eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4886afc0-1e48-5f50-950a-a5c1dfc41d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-139992" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/1f22e58fb007553116df6049bbc7cf65c9ce2c84c4846e36258e682be4cc183b.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$, while ${i}^{′}$ and ${j}^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that \n${i}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a910da25-1764-5e7e-882b-95cfc187c061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345253} {:answer/id #uuid "177c704d-c85b-5bbc-8b89-0b79b01c6fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345254} {:answer/id #uuid "63424a80-baa4-5c8d-b93c-d8d0ffa93c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345255} {:answer/id #uuid "18a4a93e-e19b-52a3-88a1-339a2ed152dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345256} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2b6d7d-3b43-50b0-b6b4-ca942e85bbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63424a80-baa4-5c8d-b93c-d8d0ffa93c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345255} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ae929d-1aaa-54d0-a2ee-43b2776f9aa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11d54815-a100-532f-83d8-b6e42a7e21f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139993" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c48366e5-892a-5332-9c06-917b851d91df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260} {:answer/id #uuid "32bb5682-5747-564c-892d-2634dd4566d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261} {:answer/id #uuid "e31ebeaa-d381-5f94-a321-685cd15b8606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345262} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b017db-3db5-5668-8785-5d1b7365f572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345263} {:answer/id #uuid "9d124221-0f5e-5653-886c-5771dfe27548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b017db-3db5-5668-8785-5d1b7365f572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345263} :answer-field/id #uuid "58a746a5-c4cd-5c9b-a06b-1f73ad47b640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "893af612-df42-5399-b5a5-36237571f3e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-43836" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3111a14f-7855-54b0-8e48-faf5e8362c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345267} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0e19c4-558e-5204-8355-5e7c2aebf89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345268} {:answer/id #uuid "52e11aa3-d859-55e1-9508-ff8cf30ddd41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345269} {:answer/id #uuid "1a066712-0015-5558-8a51-df4ab78bfda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b548fb3-08bb-58d7-8fc9-06ec6c0318c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a066712-0015-5558-8a51-df4ab78bfda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c3f61a-4fbe-5e13-b205-9c6e30182966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dc8d6e7-0a45-5ad2-ab8b-ad2aa66146a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43892" :question/problem "Given that ${i,j}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,4⟩$ and $⟨5,-1⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e0ddc0-66d3-55d2-93ad-6e49404acd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132579} {:answer/id #uuid "f822649d-1c26-5d6c-a453-4e281dba3695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132580} {:answer/id #uuid "13615556-fa13-507d-9a41-e2520e9e127d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132581} {:answer/id #uuid "254ccc77-345f-571d-b215-75ea26d48461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132582} {:answer/id #uuid "3001a438-a507-5630-93f7-a548cb31f718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13615556-fa13-507d-9a41-e2520e9e127d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132581} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66081e9-c5ef-564f-b3c4-27cf0e332a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e98d2b13-3a52-5439-8add-029a6bea2d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43894" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/5aaf29ab4dd2de3d104ca37a71ee66078f985b908465a77ca06ba8ffadd50210.png)\n\nIn the diagram above,  $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$, while ${i}^{′}$ and  ${j}^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d7fc15d-6235-5719-a726-62e36e543b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274} {:answer/id #uuid "7e418740-92c2-5424-a88b-7ac193756c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345275} {:answer/id #uuid "23b7faf1-9673-5431-8d16-e632d038cce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345276} {:answer/id #uuid "1d834e4d-0bcc-577a-8d32-ef24bdbb0561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db